<h1>Are Antares Tires Any Good? <span class="accent">An Honest Review</span></h1>

<p class="subhead">Short answer: yes, for what they are. Antares is a budget tire, and I'm not going to tell you it's a Michelin. But if you're choosing between a fresh set of Antares and a worn-out used tire — or a bald one with the cords showing — the Antares wins every time. Here's the straight version, from a shop that actually sells them.</p>

<p>I sell Antares because there's a real demand for an honest budget tire, and most "reviews" you'll find online are written by people who've never mounted one. So let me give it to you straight: what they are, who should buy them, and who shouldn't.</p>

<h2>What Antares Actually Is</h2>

<p>Antares is a budget brand made in Qingdao, China, and distributed in North America by Horizon Tires. It's traceable, it carries a real treadwear warranty (45,000 miles on most passenger and SUV sizes), and the lineup covers the sizes most people actually drive on. That matters — this isn't some no-name tire with no maker behind it and no warranty if it lets you down. It's a legitimate, warrantied, new tire at a budget price.</p>

<p>The models I stock most:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Polymax 4S</strong> — all-weather, and it's 3PMSF rated (the mountain-snowflake), so it's legal and capable for our winters. This is the one I reach for on car and CUV walk-ins.</li>
  <li><strong>Ingens A1</strong> — a quiet, comfortable all-season for cars. Good value pick for daily driving.</li>
  <li><strong>Majoris R1</strong> — all-season for bigger CUVs and crossovers.</li>
  <li><strong>Goliath AT</strong> — an all-terrain for trucks that actually looks the part, for a lot less than the name-brand ATs.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Honest Pros and Cons</h2>

<div class="cols">
  <div class="col good">
    <h3>Where they're good</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>New rubber, full tread life ahead of it</li>
      <li>Balance up easily - smooth, shimmy-free ride</li>
      <li>Real warranty (45k miles, most sizes)</li>
      <li>3PMSF winter rating on the Polymax 4S</li>
      <li>Quiet and comfortable for normal driving</li>
      <li>Will pass a safety, no problem</li>
      <li>Cheap — often around $90–150 a tire</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="col bad">
    <h3>Where they're not</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Not a premium wet-braking tire</li>
      <li>Won't match a Michelin for ultimate grip</li>
      <li>Tread life is decent, not exceptional</li>
      <li>Quality can vary a touch batch to batch</li>
      <li>Not a high-km highway-warrior tire</li>
      <li>Not a track or performance tire</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<p><strong>One more thing from the bay, and it's the part I like best:</strong> Antares balance up beautifully. I've installed plenty of them, and they take hardly any wheel weight to true up. That tells you the tire is round and built consistently - the cheap junk usually fights you and needs a fistful of weights. In plain terms, an easy balance means a smooth ride with no shimmy in the wheel. You only really notice it after mounting a lot of tires, and these just go on nice.</p>

<h2>Who Should Buy Antares</h2>

<p>This is the part that matters, because a tire is only "good" or "bad" relative to what you need it for. Antares is the right call if:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>You're choosing between used and new.</strong> This is the big one. A new Antares with full tread and a warranty beats a half-worn used set with no warranty, almost every time, for not much more money.</li>
  <li><strong>You drive normal local distances</strong> around Chatham-Kent and aren't piling on highway kilometres.</li>
  <li><strong>It's a second vehicle, a commuter, or a budget build</strong> where you don't want to sink premium money.</li>
  <li><strong>You need a safe, legal set on the car now</strong> without spending $1,000.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Who Should Spend More</h2>

<p>I'd point you up the ladder if:</p>

<ul>
  <li>You're doing <strong>30,000+ highway km a year</strong> — the longer-life premium tire wins on cost-per-kilometre.</li>
  <li><strong>Maximum wet braking is your priority</strong> — long fast commutes, a heavy vehicle. Step up to a value-tier or premium tire. (More on that in <a href="/do-you-need-premium-tires">do you really need premium tires</a>.)</li>
  <li>You want the most engineering for the money in the middle of the market — see the <a href="/value-tire-brands">value brands built by the giants</a>.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
  <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>The bottom line on Antares:</strong> it's not pretending to be a premium tire — it's a new, warrantied, budget tire that walks all over a used or bald one. If you're shopping to save money, get us to price a fresh Antares set against whatever else you're looking at. Most people are surprised how affordable new actually is. We install all four for <strong>$25 a tire</strong> right here in Pain Court.</p>
</div>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<div class="faq">
  <h4>Are Antares tires safe?</h4>
  <p>Yes. They're new, warrantied, traceable tires that meet the standards and will pass a safety inspection. They're a budget tire, not a premium one — but "budget" and "unsafe" aren't the same thing. A fresh Antares is far safer than the worn-out used tire it usually replaces.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <h4>Where are Antares tires made?</h4>
  <p>They're made in Qingdao, China, and distributed in North America by Horizon Tires. They carry a treadwear warranty (45,000 miles on most passenger/SUV sizes).</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <h4>How long do Antares tires last?</h4>
  <p>Most passenger and SUV sizes carry a 45,000-mile (about 72,000 km) treadwear warranty. Real-world life depends on your driving and rotations. It's solid budget life — not premium-tire mileage, but plenty for the price.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <h4>Are Antares good in winter?</h4>
  <p>The Polymax 4S is an all-weather tire with the 3PMSF mountain-snowflake rating, so it's legal and capable for Ontario winters as a year-round tire. For serious ice and deep snow, a dedicated winter tire still wins — but as a do-it-all budget tire, the Polymax 4S holds its own.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <h4>Antares vs a used tire — which is better?</h4>
  <p>New Antares, almost every time. A used tire is somebody else's wear with no warranty; a new Antares gives you full tread, a fresh date code, and a warranty for not much more. Unless the used set is nearly new and a steal, go new.</p>
</div>

<div class="faq">
  <h4>Do you sell Antares in Chatham-Kent?</h4>
  <p>Yes — we stock the popular car, CUV and truck sizes and can order others. Give us your tire size (it's on the door-jamb sticker, like 225/65R17) and we'll price a set and install it locally.</p>
</div>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

<p>Are Antares tires any good? For a budget tire, yes — and against the used or bald tire they usually replace, they're a clear upgrade. Don't buy them expecting Michelin performance. Do buy them if you want a safe, new, warrantied set without spending premium money. That's exactly the job they're built for, and they do it well.</p>

